ere  i also have my sauce i'm using soy sauce  black vinegar  and some chili oil i love my dumplings  super saucy so i'm going to take my  sauce  pour it all over my dumplings and  top it with some cilantro our two minute  dumplings are done they look amazing but  you know what let's give them a try  cheers that filling is so good super  flavorful that sauce really brings it  all together  make sure you don't overcook the wrapper  or also get really gummy  for two minutes this is good but if you  have a little bit more time and want  something great stick around for our  next recipe  all right 20-minute crispy pork  dumplings i  love these so let's start by making our  filling i'm going to start with some  cabbage  some pork ginger scallions  soy sauce dark soy sauce sesame oil  shaoxing wine this is very important to  put in your filling it helps cut through  that like porkiness  of the meat and if you don't have this  you might find it to be a little  porky some cornstarch half an egg  the cornstarch will just help bind it  along with our egg and  a pinch of salt now i'm just gonna mix  all this together  our filling is done let's get to  assembling so i have my wrapper here  just scooping some filling placing it in  the center  i'm going to take some water you saw me  do this in the last one  now i'm going to take the center on the  bottom match it with the top and i'm  going to  fold this outer edge in and then as we  come to a close i'm going to fold this  outer  fold well and just pinching on the sides  now i like to take this part  and curl it towards me just a little bit  and now we're gonna repeat on the other  side  i think if you're making dumplings for  the first time  this might feel a little bit stressful  but after a few you'll get it down and  you'll have yourself a cute little  dumpling and then i'm putting it on a  lightly floured  sheet tray here if your workstation is  super warm the wrappers can get a little  soggy and start to stick so i like to  have it lightly floured so i'm going to  keep going until i've made 24 dumplings  my dumpling babies are done i'm ready to  pan fry these for that crispy bottom let  me go get my pan  it's time to cook the pot stickers i  have some oil on my pan here so i'm just  going to place dumplings all around the  pan  i like to do a circular motion you want  to avoid overcrowding the pan because  you don't want them to stick together  and also you want to avoid cooking them  unevenly  right when i put them down i like to  move them just a little bit just to make  sure the bottoms aren't sticking so once  the bottoms of your dumplings are golden  brown  like this we can go ahead and add some  water  to finish cooking it with some steam so  this is another reason why you don't  want your pan to be super hot because  once you add your water into the oil it  can splatter  and it hurts speaking from experience so  once these are done  i'm going to finish the second batch and  we will be very close to eating  these are about done i'm ready to eat  look how beautifully crispy and golden  brown it is  our dumplings are ready let's go ahead  and plate them it smells  so freaking good i'm just fanning them  out  leaving some of the crispy sides up and  that'll help keep them  nice and crispy i'm just gonna sprinkle  some scallions on top  and some toasted sesame seeds i'm gonna  dip my dumpling in my sauce to make the  sauce i whisk together chili oil  low-sodium soy sauce black vinegar  sesame oil  brown sugar and ginger so i like to  completely dunk  my dumpling in the sauce and after the  first bite of my pot sticker  i dunk it again and let the sauce  infiltrate the entire  dumpling wrapper that crispy bottom  has a great crunch the bounciness of the  wrapper  and the filling has like the perfect  amount of flavor these are so good  i am going to finish all of them but  again stick around for the next one  we're making shalom ball  two days if you got two days can we cut  for like five minutes so i can just  really finish this old  plate onto our final recipe of the day  this one takes two days but is seriously  a showstopper  these are soup dumplings we call them  shalom bao translated it's  little basket bun we're gonna start by  making the pork broth which actually  takes the whole first day but it's worth  it to get started i already have some  boiled pork neck and pork skin  for this i've added my pork neck and my  pork skin  covered it with water about two inches  above  i brought it to a boil strained it and  rinsed it with cold water  this will help our broth gelatinize  so we can easily incorporate it into our  soup dumpling i'm gonna add  water ginger scallion rice wine  and peppercorn all of these mixed  together will help enrich the flavor  the rice wine and ginger will help cut  through that porkiness  so a lot of recipes for soup dumplings  actually use gelatin as a shortcut but  we're using  pork neck and pork skin because it has  natural gelatin in it you can totally  use gelatin if you want but today we're  going to go the more traditional route  now we're just going to let it come to a  boil we'll let it simmer for a couple  hours  occasionally skimming the top alright  now i'm just going to strain them so  that we get a very smooth and clean  broth  we're going to let this cool and set it  in the fridge overnight all right now  it's time to make our pork filling i'm  adding some ground pork  ginger scallions soy sauce  sesame oil rice wine sugar and lastly  some water  now i'm just gonna mix this together it  already smells so good  uh yes it's day two i'm wearing the same  shirt because we had  pork broth prepared katie didn't want to  stay overnight i don't want you back  tomorrow  all right this filling is done we're  going a little extra if you want to know  how to make dough from scratch that's  what we're doing next  first i'm adding some flour salt and  some warm water  i personally like to mix with chopsticks  wh